{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1785443136,"nonce":"6pG1uG3V7vZXT1UtKDYrpe8tlhQ=","title":"A persistent workspace drain failure holds the Pod open until the step timeout instead of naming the cause on the Attempt","message":"Found while reviewing ADR-0064 s1 against its own acceptance criterion \"a flush failure fails the Attempt and names the cause\". On the control-plane path that criterion is unmet today, and not because of anything the slice changed.\n\n`drive_workspace`'s `cas.ingest` error becomes `DriveErr::Transient` (`crates/scarab-executor-k8s/src/lib.rs:673`, `:1202-1206`) and is returned as `ExecError::Other` at `:1537-1539`, which exits `poll` **before** `settled_state` is consulted at `:1564`. So a *persistent* drain failure — a dead cold tier, a full disk, a bad bucket — loops the poll for the whole step budget and then fails as a timeout-shaped `Infra` error.\n\nTwo consequences, and the second is the worse one:\n\n1. The operator gets a timeout, not \"the workspace could not be archived because X\". The cause is in the server log and nowhere on the Attempt.\n2. The Step's side effects have already happened. Retrying a side-effecting Step because its *evidence* could not be stored is at-least-once execution of something the author may have written assuming at-most-once.\n\nA classification is wanted: a drain failure that is not going to succeed on retry should fail the Attempt promptly and carry its cause, rather than being indistinguishable from a slow Pod.\n\n- [ ] A persistent drain failure fails the Attempt without waiting out the step budget\n- [ ] The Attempt carries the cause, legibly, not just the server log\n- [ ] Transient and permanent drain failures are distinguished, and the permanent one does not present as a timeout","files":null}]}